<PageLayout
  title="Corrections Policy | Cooked Finance"
  description="How Cooked Finance receives, verifies, corrects, and discloses factual, mathematical, source, safety, and privacy errors."
  path="/corrections-policy/"
  eyebrow="Corrections"
  heading="Errors should become visible improvements."
  lead="Send the page, disputed passage or behavior, and strongest available source. Safety and calculation errors receive priority."
  updated="August 31, 2026"
>
  <h2>What counts as a material correction</h2><ul><li>A wrong formula, unit, result, label metric, or test assumption</li><li>Unsafe, incomplete, or overconfident maintenance guidance</li><li>An incorrect source interpretation, citation, review date, or recall statement</li><li>A privacy or storage behavior that differs from the disclosure</li><li>A broken route or interaction that prevents the promised task</li></ul>
  <h2>How to report an issue</h2><p>Use the <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>. Include the URL, what appears wrong, the expected correction, and a primary source when possible. Do not include account passwords, access codes, sensitive financial figures, or unnecessary household details.</p>
  <h2>Review process</h2><p>We reproduce the issue, inspect the calculation or cited source, classify its impact, and correct the page, shared data, or engine. A shared-data problem is checked across every generated page that uses it.</p>
  <h2>Disclosure</h2><p>Material changes update the substantive review date and, when they affect an engine or decision model, its version history. Minor spelling and formatting repairs may be made silently.</p>
  <h2>Intentional model changes</h2><p>A deliberate new assumption is documented in the relevant <a href="/methodology/">methodology</a>. It is not disguised as a factual correction.</p>
</PageLayout>
